1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
And now, to you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,000
David, is there anything that we missed that you'd like to tell people about?

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Uh, not that I can think of.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:16,000
You're going to get your eagle, you're going to state it publicly,

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,000
you're going to get your eagle before the HF is.

6
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:19,000
I am going to get my eagle.

7
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:22,000
I have one more meeting for a merit badge,

8
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,000
and then I'm done with all the required merit badges,

9
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:26,000
and that's the last requirement I have left.

10
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:28,000
So, I'm right there.

11
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Alright, let's see how good I am, alright?

12
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,000
It's an eagle-required merit badge.

13
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,000
You have one more meeting left for it, and it is family life.

14
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:36,000
No, it is not.

15
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Alright, wait a minute, I've got another chance.

16
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,000
It's got to be a 90-day wonder.

17
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Yeah, it's got to be personal management.

18
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Nope.

19
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Oh, man.

20
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Alright, David, what is it?

21
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Oh, it's for citizenship in the community.

22
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Oh, come on.

23
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,000
So, you were a good boy, and you did family life

24
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,000
and personal management a long time ago.

25
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:56,000
I did, actually.

26
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Okay.

27
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Alright, well...

28
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Did you fill out your Boyce Gear Eagle Scout workbook?

29
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,000
I have completed that.

30
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,000
I'm going to let you know when David finishes his

31
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Scoutmaster Conference for Eagles.

32
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Okay.

33
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,000
David, do you have enough people breathing down your neck yet?

34
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Or not?

35
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:13,000
I mean...

36
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:17,000
I'm getting to the limit.

37
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Yeah.

38
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:21,000
Hey, this is podcast number 83.

39
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Hey, welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast.

40
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:51,000
This is Clark Green.

41
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:54,000
You're going to hear a little bit more from the fellas

42
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:58,000
that we heard from just a moment ago before the theme.

43
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Later on in the podcast, it was part of an interview

44
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:02,000
that I conducted, but more about that later.

45
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,000
Let's take a look and see who's been in touch

46
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:05,000
over the past week.

47
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:07,000
I heard this from Mark Craner.

48
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Mark is a Scoutmaster down in Texas,

49
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:13,000
and he wrote a post on the blog this week

50
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,000
at scoutmastercg.com that talked about opportunities,

51
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:17,000
and he said,

52
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:19,000
That post really hit home with me, Clark.

53
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,000
As a Scoutmaster, I often find myself concentrating

54
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:24,000
on the few Scouts missing out,

55
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:27,000
as if I failed to bring them into the fold.

56
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:28,000
You're very right.

57
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,000
We need to encourage and then step back

58
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:32,000
and let Scouts make their decisions.

59
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:34,000
It's such a joy to see Scouts who are first reluctant

60
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:36,000
find their way back into Scouting,

61
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:38,000
and so on to do great things.

62
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Thanks for this post.

63
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:41,000
It was just what I needed to hear.

64
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:43,000
Well, thanks for being in touch, Mark.

65
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:46,000
I'm glad that you found that useful.

66
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:50,000
Frank Maynard was in touch about last week's podcast,

67
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:53,000
podcast number 82, in which we talked a little bit

68
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:55,000
about discipline in Scouting,

69
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:57,000
and we also talked about Scoutmaster conferences

70
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:00,000
in response to a couple of emails we had received,

71
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:02,000
and he said this podcast was chock-full

72
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:04,000
of great information for Scoutmasters.

73
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
I wish this material was covered as thoroughly

74
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:09,000
in Scoutmaster Leader-specific training.

75
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:11,000
I'm recommending it as a must-listen

76
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,000
for our troops, Scoutmaster,

77
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:15,000
and our assistant Scoutmasters.

78
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Well, thanks, Frank.

79
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:18,000
Once again, it's always good to hear from folks

80
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:20,000
and that they're finding the information

81
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,000
that we're putting out useful.

82
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:25,000
I also heard from Josh Ney.

83
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:27,000
Now, Josh is a Scoutmaster of Troop 44

84
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:29,000
in Glen Olden, PA,

85
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,000
and he said, hello there.

86
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:32,000
I subscribe to your blog,

87
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:34,000
and I thoroughly enjoy your posts.

88
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:36,000
I noticed that you are not all that far away

89
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:38,000
in southeastern Pennsylvania.

90
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:39,000
I'm about 40 minutes away,

91
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,000
and I hope to meet you sometime at an event in our area.

92
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:43,000
Well, Josh, the feeling's mutual.

93
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:45,000
We're not in the same council.

94
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:47,000
We're not in the same county.

95
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:48,000
No, right?

96
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:49,000
You could run into somebody.

97
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:50,000
It's always good to hear from folks,

98
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:52,000
whether you're from across the country

99
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:55,000
or from the next county away.

100
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:56,000
Do be in touch,

101
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:58,000
and you can email me, Clark Green,

102
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:02,000
at ScoutmasterCG at Verizon.net.

103
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Now, this is that time of year

104
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:06,000
when the fall program

105
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:07,000
is getting ready to start

106
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:09,000
in our packs and troops and crews.

107
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:10,000
I know that our troop

108
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:12,000
has got a full slate of activities,

109
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:14,000
and we've got some, you know,

110
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:16,000
council and district stuff coming up,

111
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,000
so we're going to have a very busy,

112
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:20,000
and I'm sure satisfying, fall and spring.

113
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:22,000
But let me ask you this question.

114
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:24,000
What are you going to do next summer?

115
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:25,000
What are your plans?

116
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:27,000
You know, most of us will probably end up

117
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:28,000
going for a week at Scout Camp

118
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:29,000
and everything like that,

119
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:34,000
but how about an additional adventure?

120
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:37,000
And that's the focus of our podcast this week.

121
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:39,000
Out there in Sunnyvale, California

122
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:42,000
is a troop, troop number 466,

123
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:44,000
and they have an assistant scout master

124
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:45,000
named Mike Malone,

125
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:49,000
who has put together just some really amazing

126
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:52,000
once in a lifetime adventures for their scouts.

127
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:54,000
I had the opportunity to sit down with Mike

128
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:57,000
and a life scout from their troop, David,

129
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:59,000
and we had an interview

130
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:01,000
and we talked for a while about the adventure

131
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:02,000
that they went on this summer,

132
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:04,000
which was a recreation of part

133
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:06,000
of the Lewis and Clark expedition

134
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,000
out in Montana,

135
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:11,000
and talked a little bit about how you make that happen

136
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:12,000
and what the youth role is

137
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:14,000
in making stuff like that happen

138
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:18,000
and it's meant to inspire you to start thinking big

139
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:21,000
and to start thinking about that once in a lifetime adventure

140
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:23,000
that you're going to create

141
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:27,000
in the coming months and maybe years.

142
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,000
And as you'll hear Mike say,

143
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:30,000
you just need to have the brass,

144
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,000
you have to have the moxie

145
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:34,000
to say, hey, we're going to make this happen

146
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:36,000
and you get people on board

147
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:39,000
and then, wow, you can do some pretty amazing things.

148
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:41,000
And we're going to talk all about that here

149
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:43,000
in just a moment

150
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:45,000
and that's going to take up the rest of the podcast.

151
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,000
So let's get started, shall we?

152
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:10,000
I've got a couple of guests joining us

153
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,000
on the Scoutmaster podcast today

154
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:14,000
and I think you're going to be very interested

155
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:16,000
in the story they have to tell.

156
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,000
Mike Malone has been involved in scouting

157
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:20,000
since he joined as a boy

158
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:22,000
way back in 1965

159
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:25,000
and he went on to become a 13-year-old Eagle Scout

160
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:28,000
and for those of you guys who go way, way back,

161
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:30,000
Mike was a chapter commander

162
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:32,000
in the Knights of Dunamis,

163
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:36,000
which was a precursor to the National Eagle Scout Association.

164
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:38,000
So if you remember the Knights of Dunamis,

165
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,000
you go way, way back.

166
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:42,000
We're almost all dead now.

167
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:45,000
Well, you're still kicking.

168
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:47,000
You're still doing pretty good, Mike.

169
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:50,000
Mike returned to scouting with his older son in 2001

170
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:53,000
and since then has served as an assistant scoutmaster

171
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:57,000
for Troop 466 in Sunnyvale, California.

172
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:00,000
And I've got him talking to us today

173
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:02,000
about organizing and leading

174
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:05,000
some once-in-a-lifetime adventures.

175
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:06,000
How you doing, Mike?

176
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:07,000
I'm good. How are you?

177
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:09,000
I'm doing okay.

178
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:13,000
We also managed to get David Bauer,

179
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:16,000
who is a life scout in Troop 466.

180
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:18,000
Still a life scout, David?

181
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:19,000
Yes, I am.

182
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:20,000
Almost there?

183
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:23,000
I actually have one last requirement for Eagle

184
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:25,000
and then I'll be done with that.

185
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:29,000
And you are 17 and 10 months?

186
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:30,000
Yes, 17.

187
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:32,000
And 10 months?

188
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:34,000
Yeah, about that.

189
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:36,000
Right around there.

190
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:38,000
See, I didn't even have to...

191
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:40,000
That's just...

192
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:44,000
That's 25 years of experience talking right there, folks.

193
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:46,000
I knew that somehow.

194
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:52,000
So, David, tell me about this once-in-a-lifetime adventure this summer.

195
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:56,000
Well, we flew out to Great Falls, Montana.

196
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:59,000
Went to the Montana State Fair

197
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:02,000
and we did a flag ceremony at the State Fair.

198
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:04,000
Just spent a couple of days in Great Falls

199
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:07,000
and then on Sunday, the boat,

200
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:10,000
the replica of the Lewis and Clark Bat-Toe was delivered to us

201
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:13,000
and we loaded that up on a trailer

202
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:17,000
and on Monday, we transported it out to a campsite right on the river

203
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:20,000
where we met up with some Lewis and Clark re-enactors

204
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:24,000
who taught us some basics like on how to make fires

205
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:26,000
and what they ate when they were out on the trail

206
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:27,000
and things like that.

207
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:29,000
And so we camped that night

208
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:32,000
and then we spent the next couple of days

209
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:35,000
on the river in canoes and on the boat

210
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:41,000
and we did a total of over 50 miles in three days on the river

211
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:45,000
and each day we stopped at one place for lunch

212
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:48,000
and would do a little side hike or something like that.

213
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:52,000
And then on the last day when we got out of the river

214
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,000
then we drove back to Great Falls

215
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:57,000
and the next day we did a handing-over ceremony

216
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:00,000
for the Lewis and Clark Interpretive Center

217
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:02,000
and we donated them the Bat-Toe.

218
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:06,000
Over the next couple of days we worked on my Eagle Project

219
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:10,000
which was to build a stand and display for the boat

220
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:13,000
and that ended on Saturday

221
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:17,000
and that was pretty much it. It was really fun.

222
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:20,000
So tell me about this Bat-Toe.

223
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:22,000
Describe it to me.

224
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:25,000
It was a 25 foot long keel boat

225
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:28,000
completely period accurate material so it's all wood

226
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:33,000
and it had a one square sail that we put up and down on the mast

227
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:37,000
which the mast also went up and down to aid for transporting it

228
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:40,000
and had room for four people to row

229
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:43,000
and one person on an oar in the back to help steer

230
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:47,000
and the entire thing weighed over 900 pounds.

231
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:53,000
So where in the world does a person find a Bat-Toe anymore?

232
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:57,000
We had it made from the Buffalo Maritime Center in New York.

233
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:59,000
Wow.

234
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:02,000
So once in a lifetime?

235
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:04,000
Definitely.

236
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:07,000
What do you most remember from this trip?

237
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:11,000
I'll probably remember most detail but later on will be

238
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:15,000
when the last group in the Bat-Toe missed the takeout

239
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:18,000
on the final day on the river and being a couple other scouts

240
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:21,000
we ended up running down the bank of the river

241
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:24,000
and jumping into the river and dragging it back upstream

242
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:27,000
for a quarter mile and then holding it in the water

243
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:30,000
next to the takeout while we tried to get it loaded up

244
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:32,000
under the trailer for probably half an hour.

245
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:34,000
Yeah, just 900 pounds.

246
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,000
Yeah, only 900 pounds.

247
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:38,000
Yeah, no big deal. You got that, right?

248
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,000
Of course.

249
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,000
And what river were you on?

250
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:44,000
The Missouri River.

251
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:47,000
And so obviously you guys were recreating a piece

252
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:50,000
of the Lewis and Clark expedition.

253
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:52,000
Yes.

254
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:54,000
Did you acknowledge about it before you went?

255
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:57,000
Prior to the trip, the only things I knew about Lewis and Clark

256
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:00,000
were stuff I'd learned in school and things like that.

257
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:03,000
But once I learned about what this trip was going to be focused on,

258
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:06,000
I watched a documentary and did some research

259
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:09,000
on what Lewis and Clark did when they were in the area,

260
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:12,000
like that stretch of the river that we were going to be on

261
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:16,000
just to kind of see how their trip went during that portion.

262
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:19,000
And how did their trip go? About like yours?

263
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
Well, they spent over a month

264
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:24,000
portaging around all the falls and great falls,

265
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:27,000
and we just did a float trip a little north of that.

266
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
There was a lot harder than ours was,

267
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
but we were going downstream, too,

268
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,000
so it would have been on the way back from their journey.

269
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:37,000
And on the way back, they were doing upwards of 60 miles in a day,

270
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:41,000
so that was completely different than what we were doing,

271
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:44,000
because we were doing usually between 10 and 15,

272
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:47,000
and completely different gear and stuff.

273
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:51,000
But it was just interesting because we were on the same river

274
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:54,000
that they were on, and we stayed at some of the same campsites

275
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:58,000
that they stayed at, so it was like in touch with history.

276
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:00,000
And how many people were on the trip?

277
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:04,000
I believe 27 people, including Dad.

278
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:06,000
That's a pretty good size group.

279
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:08,000
This is one of a series of four annual adventures

280
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:10,000
that Troop 466 has done,

281
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:13,000
and, Mike, you've been involved in planning all of those

282
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:15,000
and putting those together,

283
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:19,000
and you put lots of links in the post that contains this podcast

284
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:22,000
so people can learn about them in more detail,

285
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
because they really are fantastic.

286
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,000
What was the first one, Mike?

287
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:30,000
We traveled to the UK, and we did the Wainwright Walk,

288
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:35,000
which was a walk created by a rather eccentric English gentleman.

289
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
And he sat down one day and said,

290
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
I wonder if I could walk all the way across this country

291
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
on existing trails and private paths.

292
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:43,000
And he created the Wainwright Walk.

293
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:47,000
I was up near the Scottish border in the Lake District

294
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:51,000
and ended up on the North Sea at Robinswood Bay,

295
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
and it's 193 miles.

296
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,000
I had a group of overachiever, older scouts.

297
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:58,000
I found myself in a position of being an assistant scout master

298
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,000
in charge of the older scouts.

299
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,000
We have something called a bank patrol,

300
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:05,000
and these are all scouts that are typically

301
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:07,000
have been patrol leaders.

302
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:09,000
They finish that, they're working towards their eagle,

303
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:11,000
they hold senior positions in the troop,

304
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:15,000
and a separate existence for them beyond the troop.

305
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
So they're still at the troop meetings,

306
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:20,000
but we have Vanguard patrol meetings at a restaurant.

307
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:22,000
They wear a suit and tie,

308
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:26,000
and we're trying to teach them more adult skills

309
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:29,000
and give them more adult experiences to keep them engaged.

310
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,000
We do a lot of air badges.

311
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
We do a lot of other things.

312
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:36,000
I was kind of driven to create something

313
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:39,000
that would be so compelling that these older kids,

314
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:42,000
and these kids are going off to Berkeley and Trinity

315
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:44,000
and UC San Diego,

316
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:46,000
give them something that would keep them engaged in the troop

317
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:48,000
now that they had their eagle.

318
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:51,000
And so this trip was just five scouts,

319
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:54,000
two dads for half the trip,

320
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
and then just me for the other half.

321
00:13:56,000 --> 00:14:00,000
And we hiked at 193 miles across the country.

322
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:02,000
It was kind of elegant camping,

323
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:05,000
because we stayed in people's houses every night.

324
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:08,000
We finished that, we went down to the World Jamboree

325
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,000
and in the midst of all that,

326
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,000
we also took a train down to the channel

327
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:14,000
and went to Brown Sea Island

328
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:16,000
for the 100th anniversary of the original...

329
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:18,000
That was 2007, then.

330
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:20,000
2007.

331
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,000
Well, that sort of set a precedent.

332
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:25,000
The next year, I felt obliged to come up with something

333
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:27,000
equally interesting.

334
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:31,000
And as it happens, I inherited a farm,

335
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:34,000
my great-grandfather's original landrush farm

336
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:37,000
just outside of Enid, Oklahoma.

337
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:41,000
I surveyed the train tracks along the Chisholm Trail

338
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
in the 1890s.

339
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:46,000
I read about Chisholm Trail cattle drives,

340
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:47,000
reenactments of them.

341
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,000
There's only been one big one.

342
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:52,000
And I contacted the woman whose father had been the cowboy on it,

343
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:54,000
and I said, can we put something together?

344
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:56,000
She got together a group of cowboys

345
00:14:56,000 --> 00:15:00,000
and a Catholic priest was the chuck wagon master

346
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:03,000
and when they brought along a chuck wagon with mules

347
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:06,000
and gathered up horses for us

348
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:09,000
rode 50 miles up the Chisholm Trail

349
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:13,000
and towards the end of the trip, we drove cattle.

350
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:16,000
And most of these boys had never been on a horse before.

351
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:19,000
And we camped on, you know, out on the stars every night

352
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:22,000
and we had guest speakers and musicians.

353
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:24,000
It was fairly arduous,

354
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:26,000
but the boys had an amazing experience.

355
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:28,000
That compelled us to do another one.

356
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:31,000
So luckily, they went to Fillmont the next year,

357
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
which brought me a little bit of time.

358
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:37,000
The subsequent year, we did a Civil War experience.

359
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
So we went to Northern Virginia

360
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:41,000
and we went out to the Balls Bluff Battlefield

361
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
and David was along on that one, too.

362
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:47,000
And a couple of his buddies were doing their Eagle projects,

363
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:52,000
in fact, and we essentially restored the Balls Bluff Battlefield.

364
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:55,000
We cleaned up the cemetery, planted new grass,

365
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
scrubbed the gravestones.

366
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:01,000
We finished that and then we were immediately taken

367
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:07,000
as involuntary enlistees into a Union unit.

368
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,000
And one night we were taken out by the Confederates

369
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:12,000
and we were captured and taken out at Skirmish.

370
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:15,000
And then we camped with some reenactors

371
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:19,000
from the California Brigade of the Pennsylvania Regiment.

372
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:21,000
And these guys came out from Pennsylvania

373
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:25,000
and we camped on the grounds of an antebellum mansion.

374
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:28,000
And we practiced drills and fired rifles

375
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:30,000
and learned all about that.

376
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:33,000
We went up to Gettysburg, had a college professor

377
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:37,000
and authored, gave us a long lecture on the California Brigade

378
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:39,000
during the Battle of Gettysburg.

379
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,000
We walked to Pickett's Charge.

380
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:44,000
I should mention we began the trip by marching in the parade

381
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
on the Capitol Mall.

382
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,000
We finished at the National Jamboree

383
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
where in our Civil War uniforms,

384
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:54,000
we conducted the flag ceremony on VIP Day

385
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:58,000
and one of our scouts handed the Chief Scout Executive

386
00:16:58,000 --> 00:16:59,000
a big photo of the day.

387
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,000
So that was quite the finish, too.

388
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:05,000
So, David, you got to do the trip to Leesburg and everything.

389
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:06,000
Yes, I was on that one.

390
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,000
What do you remember from that one?

391
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:12,000
One thing that always stuck out was the heat

392
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:17,000
when we were doing the first parade

393
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:18,000
when we first entered.

394
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:21,000
It was just the whole...

395
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:24,000
Being in full Class A and 90 degree weather

396
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:27,000
and then 90% humidity was not that great.

397
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:31,000
But some guy did do the parade in a bomb suit

398
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
because they had Eagle Scouts

399
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:35,000
that were supposed to dress as their profession

400
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:38,000
and he was a U of D guy in the military

401
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
so he did the parade in a bomb suit

402
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:43,000
and then I kind of felt lame afterwards looking at him.

403
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:46,000
You know, Mike, where do you even get started

404
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:47,000
with something like this?

405
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:51,000
I think a lot of people have these kind of ideas,

406
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,000
maybe that this would be a great thing

407
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:55,000
to be able to do some kind of adventure,

408
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
but few of them come to reality.

409
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:58,000
What elements have to be there

410
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,000
to make something like this happen?

411
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:03,000
I've turned into a fairly systematic process at this point,

412
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:06,000
but there's always a large element of serendipity

413
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:08,000
and the unexpected.

414
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:12,000
So I feel like the Scouts have always needed

415
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
that historic dimension.

416
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
So I'm very interested in giving the boys

417
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:19,000
some sort of contextual experience

418
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:22,000
of American history or world history.

419
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:25,000
And that is to say, I want them to be 40 years old

420
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:29,000
and sitting in a bar somewhere at home watching TV

421
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:32,000
and they show on the television cowboys Red River

422
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:36,000
and they're driving the cattle across the Sinron River

423
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:38,000
and they can say to themselves,

424
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:41,000
I know what that was like, I know what that felt like.

425
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:42,000
I've done it.

426
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:46,000
Each time I begin with a kernel of a notion of history

427
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,000
and then try to build out from there.

428
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:52,000
The Lewis and Clark one came really because

429
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:55,000
I used to run a magazine called 4JFAT.

430
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:58,000
Stephen Ambrose was one of my writers on occasion.

431
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:01,000
And he wrote the great Lewis and Clark book

432
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:03,000
and once he invited me, he said,

433
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:05,000
come up to me at the Montana.

434
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:08,000
Let's float down the Missouri River through the Missouri Breaks.

435
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:11,000
Unfortunately, I didn't take him up on it and he's gone now.

436
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:13,000
But it always went back in my mind.

437
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:16,000
I thought a Lewis and Clark experience would really be something.

438
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:20,000
So beginning with that, you know, none of an idea,

439
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:21,000
I began to build outwards.

440
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,000
So I began to research Lewis and Clark

441
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:24,000
and what kind of boats they had.

442
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,000
And they were using these two,

443
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
what they described in the journals as bat toes.

444
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,000
So I began looking at bat toes

445
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:33,000
and I discovered that there was a program

446
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:38,000
at the Buffalo Maritime Center of State New York

447
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
on the lake there in Lake Erie

448
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
and they built a bat toe.

449
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:45,000
So I contacted Roger Allen there

450
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:47,000
the guy who builds them.

451
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,000
And he said, yeah, love to.

452
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:51,000
Actually, an Eagle Scout and some other scouts

453
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:53,000
helped on building the boat there.

454
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:54,000
It was a perfect replica.

455
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
Now, simultaneously, I had to figure out

456
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
what service can we do?

457
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:01,000
Creating a historic trail on the river would be one thing.

458
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:03,000
And another scout on the troop took that one.

459
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:05,000
David wanted to do something physical.

460
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:07,000
He wanted to build something.

461
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:09,000
Well, I got a hold of the Lewis and Clark

462
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:11,000
Interpretive Center in Great Falls

463
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:13,000
and I said, are you guys interested

464
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:16,000
in receiving a bat toe as your new exhibit?

465
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:19,000
And they were high on the idea.

466
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:22,000
So I connected David with Roger Allen in Buffalo

467
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:25,000
and with the Lewis and Clark Interpretive Center people.

468
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:27,000
And I just sort of stepped back

469
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:29,000
from that part of the process.

470
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:31,000
And David worked out with them

471
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:35,000
how to build a cradle for the bat toe

472
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:36,000
and all of that.

473
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:38,000
And he also helped with getting the boat,

474
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:41,000
arranging it to get it to Great Falls.

475
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:43,000
But now we had all of that put together.

476
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:46,000
So the next thing was, could I get people in Great Falls

477
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,000
to help us, help the bullies learn?

478
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
And we ended up with a whole group

479
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:53,000
of Lewis and Clark re-enactors

480
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:55,000
who came out on three different occasions

481
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:57,000
and gave extended presentations

482
00:20:57,000 --> 00:20:59,000
and showed what kind of food they ate.

483
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:01,000
Trappers came and showed furs.

484
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:03,000
They showed the rifles and the pistols

485
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:05,000
and the weapons.

486
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:06,000
It was really quiet.

487
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:09,000
It was a Native American re-enactor

488
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:12,000
who came and talked about the history of the tribes there.

489
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,000
And so the boys got a real full immersion

490
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:17,000
in this before and after we went on the river.

491
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:19,000
In the river itself,

492
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:22,000
most of these boys had never been on a canoe on a river.

493
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:25,000
And the bat toe, none of us knew how to sail.

494
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:27,000
We didn't have a rudder.

495
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:29,000
It had a stern sweep and great big bore.

496
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:32,000
So we literally learned as we went along the river.

497
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:34,000
And there was no way off that river

498
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:36,000
for those 53 miles.

499
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:38,000
The river was quite spectacular.

500
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,000
I mean, we were staying, as David said,

501
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:43,000
staying in the campgrounds where Lewis and Clark camped.

502
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:46,000
And we were going down a river in a craft just like theirs.

503
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:47,000
And we learned a lot of things.

504
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:49,000
I mean, that boat didn't have much of a key on it.

505
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:53,000
So if you put the sail up and the wind came in sideways,

506
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:55,000
that boat skidded sideways.

507
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:57,000
And David was very polite not to say

508
00:21:57,000 --> 00:22:00,000
but what happened at the very end of the trip was,

509
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:03,000
I'm standing on the bank watching the bat toe

510
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:05,000
come down the river.

511
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:09,000
And I suddenly realized they're not going to bake to the bank.

512
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:12,000
And the next bout is 50 more miles ahead.

513
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,000
But they ended up, as David said,

514
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:18,000
a quarter mile down the river with David and Brandon Hayes

515
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:22,000
diving into the river and grabbing the bow rope

516
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:24,000
and throwing up on the bank.

517
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,000
And everybody slowly pulled.

518
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:28,000
Part of my original plan was for them to actually experience

519
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:31,000
what it was like to pull that bat toe up the Missouri River.

520
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:33,000
One of those serendipitous moments

521
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:36,000
where at the very end of the trip they got that experience.

522
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:38,000
I also had everybody wear period clothes.

523
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:40,000
So David, what is that like?

524
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:43,000
Well, I fell into the river on the first day.

525
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:45,000
Not actually when we were on the river,

526
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:48,000
I was getting out of my canoe and fell right into mud.

527
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:51,000
So then I was covered in mud and water

528
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:53,000
for the rest of the trip.

529
00:22:53,000 --> 00:22:57,000
But I actually wore a Coonskin hat the entire time

530
00:22:57,000 --> 00:23:00,000
which got pretty hot during the day

531
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:03,000
because it's all leather on the inside.

532
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:07,000
But I didn't have to worry about it blowing away in the wind

533
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:10,000
and then by the end of the week I was pretty used to it

534
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:12,000
and it felt kind of weird not wearing a leather hat.

535
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:16,000
You got a real taste of an authentic historical experience.

536
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:17,000
Yeah.

537
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:20,000
Not necessarily always like something you might choose to do.

538
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,000
No, it wouldn't have been something

539
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:24,000
that I probably would have thought of

540
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:26,000
if Mr. Malone hadn't suggested it.

541
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:28,000
But it ended up being fun.

542
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,000
I mean, it's one of those crazy things

543
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:32,000
where it's a real challenge

544
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:34,000
and it's not real pleasant while you're doing it

545
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:36,000
and it kind of hurts and it's hot,

546
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:38,000
but it turns out to be fun in the end.

547
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:39,000
Yeah.

548
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:41,000
So the elements for this, first of all,

549
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:43,000
Mike, you have a passion for history.

550
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:45,000
That kind of keeps you motivated

551
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:47,000
and lights your fire about doing these things.

552
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:50,000
You also build in an element of service

553
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:54,000
is something that to me would be uniquely scouting.

554
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:56,000
I don't think that would be the first thing

555
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:58,000
people would think of in putting a trip like this together,

556
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:01,000
but that is an added dimension to it

557
00:24:01,000 --> 00:24:05,000
that also is a big motivating factor.

558
00:24:05,000 --> 00:24:07,000
Hopefully, I think that's the heart of the trip.

559
00:24:07,000 --> 00:24:10,000
It begins sort of like scouting itself.

560
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:12,000
Scouting hooks you with the camping

561
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:14,000
and the knife and the fire

562
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:15,000
and all that and the ropes

563
00:24:15,000 --> 00:24:17,000
and all that and the knots and everything.

564
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:19,000
That's how scouting lures you in,

565
00:24:19,000 --> 00:24:21,000
but ultimately scouting is about service.

566
00:24:21,000 --> 00:24:23,000
And these trips are the same thing.

567
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:25,000
I mean, we have the high concept,

568
00:24:25,000 --> 00:24:27,000
which is Lewis and Clark or Civil War

569
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:29,000
or Cowboys driving cattle.

570
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:31,000
But at the heart of it,

571
00:24:31,000 --> 00:24:33,000
what they come away with is

572
00:24:33,000 --> 00:24:35,000
they've made a major contribution on it.

573
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:39,000
We restored a number of miles

574
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:40,000
of the Chisholm Trail,

575
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:43,000
repainting the old concrete posts.

576
00:24:43,000 --> 00:24:45,000
And that's now playing a crucial role

577
00:24:45,000 --> 00:24:47,000
in getting the Chisholm Trail

578
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:49,000
National Historic Trail status in Congress.

579
00:24:49,000 --> 00:24:51,000
That's what's going to be in the spring.

580
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:53,000
And they're using some of our work

581
00:24:53,000 --> 00:24:55,000
and publicity

582
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:57,000
as part of making the case for that.

583
00:24:57,000 --> 00:24:58,000
The Civil War one,

584
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:00,000
the results are going to be the reenactors

585
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:01,000
going to go out there

586
00:25:01,000 --> 00:25:04,000
and see something really historically correct

587
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:05,000
and beautiful.

588
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:08,000
And he was to say the people of Montana

589
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:10,000
just got themselves a beautiful new exhibit

590
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:11,000
at their museum.

591
00:25:11,000 --> 00:25:12,000
They're going to have a historic trail.

592
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:14,000
And David, I think I have a pretty good idea

593
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:16,000
of what attracted you to participate

594
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:17,000
in these types of things.

595
00:25:17,000 --> 00:25:19,000
What was the selling point for you?

596
00:25:19,000 --> 00:25:22,000
Basically, it's just the great summer adventure.

597
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:26,000
I hit the opportunity to go spend a week

598
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:29,000
with my friends that are in the troop

599
00:25:29,000 --> 00:25:31,000
doing some awesome trip

600
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:33,000
that Mr. Malone has put together,

601
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:36,000
something that I would never be able to do

602
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:39,000
if someone hadn't proposed it.

603
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:41,000
I never would have thought of traveling to Virginia

604
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:43,000
and reenacting the Civil War

605
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:45,000
or flying out to Montana

606
00:25:45,000 --> 00:25:49,000
to do a Lewis and Clark canoe trip.

607
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:55,000
But when the idea was proposed to me,

608
00:25:55,000 --> 00:25:57,000
it sounded like something that would be

609
00:25:57,000 --> 00:25:59,000
a great way to spend a week or two in the summer

610
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:01,000
and get a really unique experience

611
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:02,000
and not very many other people

612
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:04,000
have gotten the chance to do before.

613
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:06,000
What was the youth role in preparing

614
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:09,000
and getting everybody on the trip

615
00:26:09,000 --> 00:26:11,000
and then conducting the trip itself?

616
00:26:11,000 --> 00:26:14,000
On both trips, we had a SPL for the trip

617
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:17,000
who would basically run a lot of the things

618
00:26:17,000 --> 00:26:19,000
on the trip, but before the trip,

619
00:26:19,000 --> 00:26:21,000
we would have meetings every other week

620
00:26:21,000 --> 00:26:23,000
to figure out how the planning of the trip

621
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:25,000
is going and discuss things.

622
00:26:25,000 --> 00:26:27,000
A lot of times, it would be scouts

623
00:26:27,000 --> 00:26:29,000
that would bring up ideas

624
00:26:29,000 --> 00:26:31,000
for what gear would need to be brought

625
00:26:31,000 --> 00:26:33,000
or how food would work and things like that.

626
00:26:33,000 --> 00:26:35,000
For my Eagle project,

627
00:26:35,000 --> 00:26:39,000
I ended up doing a large bulk of the work

628
00:26:39,000 --> 00:26:41,000
for figuring out how to actually get the bateau

629
00:26:41,000 --> 00:26:43,000
from New York to Montana

630
00:26:43,000 --> 00:26:46,000
and coordinating with the Interpretive Center

631
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:48,000
and the Maritime Center

632
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:50,000
and just figuring out how we could get that there

633
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:52,000
so we could actually have the trip.

634
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:54,000
Pretty extensive preparation

635
00:26:54,000 --> 00:26:56,000
and pretty extensive work on your part

636
00:26:56,000 --> 00:26:58,000
as a youth leader in this.

637
00:26:58,000 --> 00:27:00,000
I'm adding that to my list.

638
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:03,000
So far, we've got there's passionate vision

639
00:27:03,000 --> 00:27:05,000
that just really lights somebody's fire

640
00:27:05,000 --> 00:27:08,000
and they say, wow, this would be a great thing to do.

641
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:10,000
You add to that an element of service.

642
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:12,000
I'm going to repeat myself in saying,

643
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:14,000
that's not the first thing most people would think of,

644
00:27:14,000 --> 00:27:16,000
I think that's a huge part of this.

645
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:20,000
And then you also have the element of

646
00:27:20,000 --> 00:27:23,000
involving youth in leading and planning

647
00:27:23,000 --> 00:27:25,000
and thinking it through.

648
00:27:25,000 --> 00:27:28,000
I mean, it just sounds like a wonderful thing

649
00:27:28,000 --> 00:27:30,000
to be able to do.

650
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:32,000
Let me add a couple more things to the list.

651
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:34,000
One of them is value-added experiences.

652
00:27:34,000 --> 00:27:37,000
And that is, once you've got the core trip put together,

653
00:27:37,000 --> 00:27:39,000
and once that's happened,

654
00:27:39,000 --> 00:27:42,000
I typically hand over the logistics

655
00:27:42,000 --> 00:27:44,000
to a mom or a dad.

656
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:46,000
The details are,

657
00:27:46,000 --> 00:27:48,000
because we're a boy-run troop,

658
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:50,000
the details go to this SPL

659
00:27:50,000 --> 00:27:52,000
and the scouts that are their Eagle projects.

660
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:54,000
And then I go off

661
00:27:54,000 --> 00:27:56,000
and start looking for value-added things.

662
00:27:56,000 --> 00:27:58,000
So we went to the Charlie Russell Museum.

663
00:27:58,000 --> 00:28:01,000
I found out that the State Fair was going on.

664
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:03,000
Literally when I got there,

665
00:28:03,000 --> 00:28:05,000
in Great Falls, I found out the State Fair was going on.

666
00:28:05,000 --> 00:28:07,000
So I called the PR director

667
00:28:07,000 --> 00:28:10,000
and I said, hey, you know,

668
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:12,000
we do a historical flag ceremony.

669
00:28:12,000 --> 00:28:15,000
We have seven flags that we tell this whole thing

670
00:28:15,000 --> 00:28:17,000
and unfurl the flags.

671
00:28:17,000 --> 00:28:19,000
And she said, come on over, let's do it.

672
00:28:19,000 --> 00:28:21,000
So we ended up in front of a big crowd in front of a concert

673
00:28:21,000 --> 00:28:23,000
doing a flag ceremony.

674
00:28:23,000 --> 00:28:26,000
I locate any historic trail patches,

675
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:28,000
metals that the boys can learn.

676
00:28:28,000 --> 00:28:30,000
So they earned four of them when they were going to Virginia.

677
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:32,000
I keep adding more and more stuff

678
00:28:32,000 --> 00:28:34,000
until every second of the trip is completely filled.

679
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:36,000
Some of those things turn out to be busts.

680
00:28:36,000 --> 00:28:39,000
Some of them turn out to be the best part of some of the trips.

681
00:28:39,000 --> 00:28:42,000
And the next thing is you've got to be flexible and adaptable.

682
00:28:42,000 --> 00:28:44,000
Something strange will happen.

683
00:28:44,000 --> 00:28:46,000
Something will go wrong.

684
00:28:46,000 --> 00:28:48,000
There's a lot of improvisation going on in these things.

685
00:28:48,000 --> 00:28:50,000
That's the next factor, adaptability.

686
00:28:50,000 --> 00:28:52,000
And then the final one is, and that's publicity.

687
00:28:52,000 --> 00:28:54,000
Publicity is not that hard.

688
00:28:54,000 --> 00:28:56,000
You just have to know who to call

689
00:28:56,000 --> 00:28:58,000
and figure out what your hooks are

690
00:28:58,000 --> 00:29:00,000
when we did the Chisholm Trail.

691
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:03,000
Every TV station and newspaper in Oklahoma covered this.

692
00:29:03,000 --> 00:29:06,000
And then we ended up on a cover of Scouting Magazine.

693
00:29:06,000 --> 00:29:09,000
And Boy's Life is doing a cover story

694
00:29:09,000 --> 00:29:12,000
on the Lewis and Clark trip.

695
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:16,000
So what's the next big once-in-a-lifetime adventure?

696
00:29:16,000 --> 00:29:19,000
Well, I try to keep it secret from the truth as best I can,

697
00:29:19,000 --> 00:29:22,000
in part because I want them to be surprised and thrilled

698
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:24,000
and in part because I can't guarantee

699
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:27,000
it's going to happen until right about now.

700
00:29:27,000 --> 00:29:29,000
Right, yeah.

701
00:29:29,000 --> 00:29:31,000
I'd love to do that sailing on the coast

702
00:29:31,000 --> 00:29:34,000
of the Northwest in a square rig.

703
00:29:34,000 --> 00:29:36,000
That would be an amazing experience.

704
00:29:36,000 --> 00:29:38,000
The other big one I would love to do

705
00:29:38,000 --> 00:29:40,000
and I'm going to have to figure out

706
00:29:40,000 --> 00:29:42,000
how to get one of these Silicon Valley companies

707
00:29:42,000 --> 00:29:43,000
to help sponsor it.

708
00:29:43,000 --> 00:29:45,000
I'd like to take the boys to Africa.

709
00:29:45,000 --> 00:29:47,000
I've spent a lot of time in Africa.

710
00:29:47,000 --> 00:29:49,000
My son's Eagle project, my oldest son,

711
00:29:49,000 --> 00:29:51,000
he got an Adams Award for it,

712
00:29:51,000 --> 00:29:54,000
was working with an orphanage in Zambia.

713
00:29:54,000 --> 00:29:57,000
I'd like to take the boys back to that orphanage.

714
00:29:57,000 --> 00:30:00,000
And I'd also like, toward the Hornaday,

715
00:30:00,000 --> 00:30:03,000
I'd like to reintroduce a white rhino

716
00:30:03,000 --> 00:30:06,000
or a mating pair of white rhinos to Namibia.

717
00:30:06,000 --> 00:30:08,000
I have a friend there that said,

718
00:30:08,000 --> 00:30:10,000
I'd like to bring a couple rhinos in Namibia

719
00:30:10,000 --> 00:30:12,000
and have the boys help out doing that

720
00:30:12,000 --> 00:30:15,000
then go work in this AIDS orphanage in Zambia.

721
00:30:15,000 --> 00:30:17,000
I think that's an amazing trip.

722
00:30:17,000 --> 00:30:19,000
David, does that, if I put a rope in your hand

723
00:30:19,000 --> 00:30:21,000
and there's a white rhino at the other end,

724
00:30:21,000 --> 00:30:22,000
you're in, right?

725
00:30:22,000 --> 00:30:24,000
Oh, of course.

726
00:30:24,000 --> 00:30:26,000
I mean, because a white rhino

727
00:30:26,000 --> 00:30:28,000
is only a little bit bigger than a bat-toe.

728
00:30:28,000 --> 00:30:30,000
It might weigh a bit more.

729
00:30:30,000 --> 00:30:32,000
I would imagine, but that would,

730
00:30:32,000 --> 00:30:34,000
that sounds like an amazing trip to me.

731
00:30:34,000 --> 00:30:38,000
These are things of, like, immense scope

732
00:30:38,000 --> 00:30:40,000
and real vision.

733
00:30:40,000 --> 00:30:42,000
Does anybody ever come along and just tell you

734
00:30:42,000 --> 00:30:44,000
that you're nuts and this is not going to work?

735
00:30:44,000 --> 00:30:46,000
I think they've come to believe

736
00:30:46,000 --> 00:30:48,000
I can pull off miracles.

737
00:30:48,000 --> 00:30:51,000
You just kind of have a moxie to go after it.

738
00:30:51,000 --> 00:30:53,000
That's what it comes down to.

739
00:30:53,000 --> 00:30:55,000
I think I'm a Silicon Valley Algae Entrepreneur at heart.

740
00:30:55,000 --> 00:30:57,000
I want you to take this the right way,

741
00:30:57,000 --> 00:30:59,000
but you're nothing special.

742
00:30:59,000 --> 00:31:01,000
I mean, this is not something that would be impossible

743
00:31:01,000 --> 00:31:02,000
for other people to do.

744
00:31:02,000 --> 00:31:04,000
Not at all, especially if you

745
00:31:04,000 --> 00:31:06,000
delegate the responsibilities

746
00:31:06,000 --> 00:31:08,000
and the scouts really carry the ball

747
00:31:08,000 --> 00:31:11,000
for the core of the trip, you know, the service.

748
00:31:11,000 --> 00:31:13,000
I mean, that's their job

749
00:31:13,000 --> 00:31:15,000
to put that together and deal with the adults.

750
00:31:15,000 --> 00:31:17,000
That's what an Eagle Project is.

751
00:31:17,000 --> 00:31:19,000
So that takes care of itself.

752
00:31:19,000 --> 00:31:21,000
You just got to have the, you know,

753
00:31:21,000 --> 00:31:22,000
the guts to go for it.

754
00:31:22,000 --> 00:31:24,000
And you're not tired of doing this yet?

755
00:31:24,000 --> 00:31:26,000
No, I find it very intriguing.

756
00:31:26,000 --> 00:31:28,000
What have the implications been for the trip?

757
00:31:28,000 --> 00:31:30,000
The word about these trips gotten out there?

758
00:31:30,000 --> 00:31:32,000
I mean, is it caused some excitement

759
00:31:32,000 --> 00:31:34,000
about Troop 466?

760
00:31:34,000 --> 00:31:36,000
I know our scout master, Dave Holt,

761
00:31:36,000 --> 00:31:38,000
he says that one of the things he hears

762
00:31:38,000 --> 00:31:40,000
of this is the reason that these parents

763
00:31:40,000 --> 00:31:42,000
are bringing their little weeblos to the 466

764
00:31:42,000 --> 00:31:44,000
is because they've heard about these trips

765
00:31:44,000 --> 00:31:46,000
and they hope one day to do it.

766
00:31:46,000 --> 00:31:48,000
If I'm going to put something like this together

767
00:31:48,000 --> 00:31:50,000
or if anybody listening begins to catch on

768
00:31:50,000 --> 00:31:52,000
and they think that this is a great idea,

769
00:31:52,000 --> 00:31:54,000
we need a little creativity.

770
00:31:54,000 --> 00:31:56,000
We need to bring it together

771
00:31:56,000 --> 00:31:58,000
with a little creativity.

772
00:31:58,000 --> 00:32:00,000
We need to brass to be able to say,

773
00:32:00,000 --> 00:32:02,000
hey, this is going to actually happen,

774
00:32:02,000 --> 00:32:04,000
so let's start moving forward.

775
00:32:04,000 --> 00:32:06,000
And then those other elements we talked about.

776
00:32:06,000 --> 00:32:08,000
This is the right time of year

777
00:32:08,000 --> 00:32:10,000
to start sparking people's ideas

778
00:32:10,000 --> 00:32:12,000
about something big for next summer.

779
00:32:12,000 --> 00:32:14,000
Mike, you're a reporter

780
00:32:14,000 --> 00:32:16,000
and an author and you're completing

781
00:32:16,000 --> 00:32:18,000
a book

782
00:32:18,000 --> 00:32:20,000
to be published in 2012

783
00:32:20,000 --> 00:32:22,000
about the history of Eagle Scouting.

784
00:32:22,000 --> 00:32:24,000
Eagle Scouting turns

785
00:32:24,000 --> 00:32:26,000
into a hundred next year.

786
00:32:26,000 --> 00:32:28,000
And is this a

787
00:32:28,000 --> 00:32:30,000
BSA book?

788
00:32:30,000 --> 00:32:32,000
It's going to be a BSA book.

789
00:32:32,000 --> 00:32:34,000
The museum and archives have opened up

790
00:32:34,000 --> 00:32:36,000
all of their photographs.

791
00:32:36,000 --> 00:32:38,000
So it should be a major, major book.

792
00:32:38,000 --> 00:32:40,000
You probably see it in every Boy Scout

793
00:32:40,000 --> 00:32:42,000
to shop next year.

794
00:32:42,000 --> 00:32:44,000
Well, hopefully as soon as it comes out

795
00:32:44,000 --> 00:32:46,000
we'll get back together.

796
00:32:46,000 --> 00:32:48,000
We'll put you on the podcast again and talk about it.

797
00:32:48,000 --> 00:32:50,000
Deal.

798
00:32:50,000 --> 00:32:52,000
I read the same feeling in your blog

799
00:32:52,000 --> 00:32:54,000
which is it's a great honor

800
00:32:54,000 --> 00:32:56,000
to work with these kids.

801
00:32:56,000 --> 00:32:58,000
When you're talking about

802
00:32:58,000 --> 00:33:00,000
kids that are on their way to Eagle

803
00:33:00,000 --> 00:33:02,000
you're really dealing with extraordinary

804
00:33:02,000 --> 00:33:04,000
young men and it's just an honor

805
00:33:04,000 --> 00:33:06,000
to be able to

806
00:33:06,000 --> 00:33:08,000
help them have great adventures.

807
00:33:08,000 --> 00:33:10,000
I'm sure they appreciate it.

808
00:33:10,000 --> 00:33:12,000
It's great work that you're doing.

809
00:33:12,000 --> 00:33:14,000
I really appreciate you guys taking the time

810
00:33:14,000 --> 00:33:16,000
and I hope we can talk again sometime.

811
00:33:16,000 --> 00:33:18,000
Yeah, thanks Clark. Take care.

812
00:33:18,000 --> 00:33:20,000
Thank you.

